{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Sarah died in Kiriath-arba.</b> Scripture informs us that this righteous woman died in the land of Israel and was buried in the land of Israel, for at her death it is written: 'the same is Hebron in the land of Canaan,' and at her burial it is written at the end of the portion: 'and afterward Abraham buried Sarah his wife… the same is Hebron in the land of Canaan.' This comes to teach of her great merit, that her death and burial were in the place where the House of Elohim is destined to be, and there the gate of heaven. And Scripture called Hebron 'Kiriath-arba' after the four pairs that were buried there, each man with his wife: Adam and Eve, Abraham and Sarah, Isaac and Rebecca, Jacob and Leah. Or after the four giants — Ahiman, Sheshai, Talmai, and their father — who dwelt there. Or because it was the allotment of the tribe of Judah, who was the fourth son." "<b>And it is possible</b> to say further concerning the name Kiriath-arba, the same is Hebron: it is called Hebron because every one buried there — his soul becomes joined on high in the city of Elohim with the four camps of the Shekhinah, and it was not for naught that the fathers of the world desired it, but because from there the souls merit to be joined to their root, which is the Throne of Glory; and this is 'Kiriath-arba, the same is Hebron.'" "<b>And Abraham came to mourn for Sarah and to weep for her.</b> If the intention were that he came from another place, Scripture would have specified and said 'and he came from such-and-such a place'; but its meaning is that he was roused to mourning for Sarah, for whoever is roused to any task whatsoever, Scripture calls him 'came to it' — and this is the language of our Rabbis of blessed memory in the Mishnah in tractate Tamid, concerning the flaying and dismemberment of the daily offering: 'he came to the left flank, he came to its tail and left in it two ribs, he came to its neck — I have not come to this measurement.' And what strengthens for you that Scripture mentioned the language of coming with a lamed is that it did not say 'ויבא אברהם ויספוד את שרה.' And the fact that it says 'to mourn for Sarah' — since she had already been mentioned by name in 'ותמת שרה,' it should have said 'to mourn her and to weep for her'; but it is the way of those who eulogize to mention the name of the deceased in their eulogy, as in the case of David (II Samuel 1:23): 'שאול ויהונתן הנאהבים והנעימים.' And it should further have said 'and to weep for her and to bury her,' for burial is the essence of lovingkindness and the culmination of mourning and weeping, and the essence of the matters toward which Abraham was roused was the burial, which is among the attributes of the Holy One, blessed be He, for He is abundant in lovingkindness; but had it said so, it would have appeared that all the matters followed one after another in immediate succession — mourning, weeping, and burial — but it was not so; rather, the mourning and the weeping were in immediate succession, but not so the burial, for he first needed to seek a place." "<b>And it seems to me</b> further that Scripture should have said: 'And Abraham and Isaac came to mourn for Sarah and to weep for her,' for certainly Isaac was fitting to mourn his righteous mother and to weep over her, since the obligation upon him was very great, even more than upon Abraham, and moreover her love for him was an exceeding love, for she bore him by way of a wonder, and her soul was bound up with his soul." "<b>But</b> it is possible to say that Isaac did not know at that time that his mother had died, for since her death was on his account — because of the news of the binding (akedah) — therefore they concealed her death from him and did not tell him. And for this reason we do not find that Scripture mentions Isaac at all, neither at her death nor at her burial; and also from the time he was bound upon the altar we have not seen him, for when Abraham and Isaac went to Mount Moriah it is written of their return (Genesis 22:19): 'וישב אברהם אל נעריו,' and it should have said 'וישובו אל הנערים' — and it does not mention Isaac's return." "<b>And it is possible</b> that he remained there on Mount Moriah for three years, until forty years were completed for him and he married Rebekah; and for this reason his return is not mentioned until the servant brought him Rebekah, and then Scripture mentions him, as it says (Genesis 24:62): ״ויצחק בא מבוא באר לחי ראי״." "<b>And in the Midrash:</b> ״ויבא אברהם״ — he came from Mount Moriah. She heard of the binding (akedah) and her soul flew out; therefore her death is juxtaposed to the perashah of the binding."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:14:31.936449229Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}